<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Urup</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Urup is a river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, about 230 kilometres long, a left tributary of the Kuban. It rises on the northern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachay-cherkessia">Karachay-Cherkessia</a> and flows north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krasnodar">Krasnodar</a> Krai. Its valley was home to the Besleney <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> and other Adyghe communities until the Russian conquest of the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/urup.jpg" alt="Urup" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Mohonu at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UrupISS002-E-8994.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
